This is a simple Python implementation of the Genetic Algorithm demonstration at www.ai-junkie.com
The original code was in C++. I felt the need of coding the thing myself, and decided to do it in Python.

The problem we solve is:
    "For any given 'target' number, find an expression invlolving any legal combination of
    addition(+), subtraction(-), multiplication(*) and division(/) on digits that represents the
    given target number.

    e.g., for the target 15, "9 + 3 * 2" is a solution."

    **Important: In Fup's code, the evaluation of the expressions generated was simple, left to right
    evaluation. I have used normal arithmetic precedence based evaluation(see `expr.py')

FX research scanner (fx_research/)
----------------------------------
The same GA idea applied to FX: every hour it labels each pair's last 7 trading
days with a regime, finds the most similar windows in the last 3 years, and
evolves fighter-entry settings (limit distance, SL/TP, re-pricing) on what
happened after them. Results go to the `fx_research` schema in Supabase.
See docs/FX_MT4_PLAN.md for how it ties into MT4-TradeSignals and TradeCoreV2.

    pip install -r requirements.txt
    python -m fx_research.scan --dry-run
    python -m fx_research.scheduler

Live execution: USDSGD 15-minute entry watch
---------------------------------------------
`fx_research.scheduler` also runs a second job every 15 minutes
(`fx_research/entry_watch.py`, on top of `fx_research/execute.py`'s
scan -> decide -> fighter-entry flow) that checks USDSGD specifically for a
fighter-entry signal and, if armed, places and manages it until it fills or
times out. A one-off pass (no scheduler) looks like:

    python -m fx_research.entry_watch                  # paper: analyse, decide, notify -- never orders
    python -m fx_research.entry_watch --allow-live      # can place a real order, only if armed (see below)
    python -m fx_research.scheduler --allow-live        # hourly scan + 15-min USDSGD watch, forever

Two safety gates both have to be open before a real order is placed, same
as `fx_research/live_runner.py`'s already-established pattern -- there is
one remote kill switch across both live-trading entry points in this repo:

1. The process itself was started with `--allow-live`.
2. `fx_research.trading_control`'s row for `USDSGD` has `enabled = true`
   and `armed_until` in the future (`sql/fx_live_schema.sql`) -- flip this
   from anywhere (the web UI, a Supabase SQL editor, curl) without
   restarting the scheduler process.

Without both, every 15-minute pass still runs the full analysis and decision
and sends the same Telegram notifications (`TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`/
`TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID` env vars, see `fx_research/scheduler.py::notify`) -- it
just never calls MT4's order-placement API. No message is sent when there's
no signal (SKIP), only when one is found and again once it resolves
(filled / expired / rejected / dry-run).

**Prerequisite**: `fx_research` reads price bars from `public.fx_prices`
(`FXR_PRICES_TABLE`), a *different* table from `public.prices` used
elsewhere in this repo -- confirm USDSGD/H1 rows tagged with your
`FXR_PRICES_SOURCE` actually exist there before expecting a live signal;
`analyse_instrument` raises a clear "not enough trading days of data" error
otherwise, it won't fail silently.

**This must run on the Windows box next to the actual MT4 terminal**
(`deploy/windows_setup.ps1`), not in a Linux sandbox: MT4 connectivity is a
ZeroMQ bridge (`EACommunicator_API`, `MT4_TRADESIGNALS_PATH`) to a running
MT4 terminal with DLL imports enabled, same-machine/same-LAN only.

===============================================================================
strategy/ -- GA-evolved long-only trading entry signals
===============================================================================

A second, unrelated use of the same genetic-algorithm idea: instead of
evolving a bit-string that decodes to an arithmetic expression, it evolves a
real-valued genome that decodes to a long-only entry rule (an AND of
indicator conditions) plus a risk model (ATR stop, R-multiple target, max
holding period). Fitness is "Edge": a Wilson-confidence-adjusted expectancy
in R-multiples, computed from realized trades in an actual bar-by-bar
backtest -- not a curve fit to a single number.

Quickstart (synthetic data, no external dependencies beyond pandas/numpy):

    python3 -m venv .venv && ./.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.run_discovery --source synthetic

This runs the GA per synthetic symbol, evolves entry rules on the first 70%
of the data, re-tests each surviving rule on the held-out last 30%
(walk-forward validation -- the only real defense against reporting a
curve-fit rule as having edge), and writes every discovered rule plus its
in-sample and out-of-sample stats to output/strategies/latest.json.
Rules that keep a positive edge out-of-sample are flagged
"validated_out_of_sample": true; most won't be, and that's expected --
finding a real, robust long-only edge is supposed to be hard.

Real data (once network access to this workspace's Supabase project is open):

    export SUPABASE_URL="https://<project>.supabase.co"
    export SUPABASE_KEY="<anon-or-service-role-key>"
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.run_discovery --source supabase \
        --symbols USDSGD,EURUSD --timeframe H1 --source-name FTMO_MT4_demo

Defaults already match this workspace's public.prices schema (Ccy,
Timeframe, Source, Datetime, Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) -- see
strategy/data.py to remap if that ever changes.

Local CSV mirror (works without network access, e.g. sandboxed environments
whose network policy blocks the Supabase host -- the situation this was
built for; also just faster for repeated runs):

    # once network access is open, mirror Supabase rows to data/prices/*.csv:
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.fetch_supabase_csv --timeframe H1 --source-name FTMO_MT4_demo

    # then run discovery against that local snapshot any time, no network needed:
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.run_discovery --source local \
        --timeframe H1 --source-name FTMO_MT4_demo

--source (synthetic/csv/supabase/local) is the one setting that switches
between local files and the live database; its default can also be set via
`export STRATEGY_SOURCE=local` instead of passing --source every time.
--symbols is optional for supabase/local -- omitted, both auto-discover
what's available (best-effort sample for supabase, directory listing for
local).

FTMO's MT4 demo feed is a "controlled" environment (limited history/
reliability) -- for a real 20+ year walk-forward and robustness test, mirror
free historical data from HistData.com instead (no signup, no API key):

    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.fetch_histdata_csv --symbol USDSGD --years 20
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.run_discovery --source local \
        --timeframe H1 --source-name histdata --symbols USDSGD

See strategy/fetch_histdata_csv.py for the timezone caveat (HistData's fixed
EST-derived UTC vs. MT4 broker time -- keep the two Source tags separate,
never concatenate them into one series) and which other instruments
HistData.com offers for free on the same terms (majors, several minors
including USD/SGD, and commodities/indices such as XAU/USD, XAG/USD,
WTI/USD, BCO/USD).

To write it straight into the same Supabase public.prices table as a new,
separately-tagged Source instead of a local file -- for direct side-by-side
comparison against the existing FTMO_MT4_demo rows -- use --dest supabase.
This assumes the Supabase key's RLS policy already permits inserts, and
does NOT de-duplicate: don't re-run it for the same symbol/range.

    export SUPABASE_URL="https://<project>.supabase.co"
    export SUPABASE_KEY="<key with insert permission on prices>"
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.fetch_histdata_csv --symbol USDSGD --years 20 \
        --dest supabase --supabase-source-name HistData_Free
    ./.venv/bin/python -m strategy.run_discovery --source supabase \
        --timeframe H1 --source-name HistData_Free --symbols USDSGD

Module layout:
    strategy/indicators.py     SMA/EMA/RSI/MACD/Bollinger/ADX/Stochastic/ATR
    strategy/genome.py         real-coded chromosome <-> entry-rule decoding
    strategy/backtest.py       long-only trade simulator (next-bar-open fills)
    strategy/fitness.py        Edge + the full backtest-basics metric set
    strategy/ga_engine.py      the GA loop itself (selection/crossover/mutation)
    strategy/interpretation.py plain-English rationale per archetype combo
    strategy/fetch_supabase_csv.py  mirrors Supabase rows to data/prices/*.csv
    strategy/fetch_histdata_csv.py  mirrors free HistData.com H1 bars to data/prices/*.csv
    strategy/slicer.py         chronological train/test split
    strategy/robustness.py     vs-random / noise / permutation overfitting checks
    strategy/data.py           Supabase / CSV / synthetic-demo data loading
    strategy/export.py         JSON schema matching the planned Supabase table
    strategy/report.py         per-strategy Markdown scorecard + run summary
    strategy/track_live.py     log live/paper trades, compare vs. backtest
    strategy/run_discovery.py  CLI that ties it all together

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Metrics computed everywhere edge is computed
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

strategy/fitness.py's compute_edge() is the single place trades turn into
numbers, and every context (GA search, in-sample, out-of-sample, robustness
variants, live/paper tracking) funnels through it -- so these are always
defined identically and comparable across environments, instruments, and
strategies:

    n_trades, meets_min_trade_count      trade count + the 30-trade
                                          statistical-significance floor
    win_rate, win_rate_lb95              raw and Wilson-95%-lower-bound win rate
    avg_win_R, avg_loss_R, reward_risk   average winner/loser size and R:R
    profit_factor                        gross win / gross loss
    net_profit_R                         sum of R multiples (risk-normalized P&L;
                                          no position sizing is modeled, so this
                                          is P&L in units of risk-per-trade, not $)
    max_drawdown_R                       worst peak-to-trough on the cumulative-R
                                          equity curve
    pnl_to_dd_ratio                      net_profit_R / max_drawdown_R
    sharpe_ratio                         annualized using trade frequency (mean/std
                                          of R multiples x sqrt(trades/year); None
                                          if entry/exit timestamps aren't usable)
    expectancy_R, edge_score             mean R/trade, and the Wilson-lower-bound-
                                          adjusted version used to rank/select

Data-splitting: every discovered rule is evolved on a 70/30 (configurable via
--train-frac) chronological train/test split and reported as both
performance.in_sample and performance.out_of_sample; validated_out_of_sample
requires OOS edge_score > 0 with >= 10 OOS trades.

Robustness/overfitting checks (strategy/robustness.py), run automatically in
run_discovery.py on strategies that already passed the walk-forward gate
(skip with --skip-robustness; tune sample sizes with --n-random/--n-noise/
--n-shuffles) and stored under record["robustness"]:

    vs_random_distribution   reruns the same risk model off of randomly timed
                              entries matched to the real rule's signal count,
                              many times over; reports the real rule's
                              percentile rank against that null distribution
                              (beats_random_at_95pct)
    noise_test                reruns the same rule against small multiplicative
                              perturbations of the OHLC data; reports the
                              (P90-P10)/median spread of edge_score
                              (robust_spread_lt_0_5, standard threshold <0.5)
    permutation_test          shuffles the bar-to-bar log-return sequence
                              (same distribution, randomized order), rebuilds a
                              synthetic OHLC path, and reruns the rule; reports
                              whether the real (ordered) edge clearly beats the
                              shuffled distribution (depends_on_real_sequence_at_95pct)

These are all in the Markdown scorecard (output/reports/<id>.md) and rolled
up as a pass-count in the run summary index (output/reports/summary_<run>.md).

Every run_discovery.py run, in addition to output/strategies/*.json, writes:
    output/reports/<strategy_id>.md          fixed scorecard: rule, risk model,
                                              interpretation, backtest stats
    output/reports/summary_<run>.md          one-line-per-strategy index
    output/live_tracking/<strategy_id>.json  seed ledger (backtest baseline +
                                              empty live_trades log)

To track real/paper trading results against a strategy's backtest baseline:

    python -m strategy.track_live --strategy-id <id> --r-multiple 1.8 \
        --entry-time 2026-09-20T14:30:00 --exit-time 2026-09-22T10:00:00 \
        --note "clean trend day"

This appends the trade to output/live_tracking/<id>.json and rewrites
output/reports/<id>_live.md with the logged trades plus a live-vs-backtest
comparison table (win rate, R:R, expectancy, edge score), computed with the
exact same Edge metric used during discovery -- so "is this still working
live" is a direct, apples-to-apples comparison, not a vibe check.
